      "## Pick a rhythm before item 40",
      "Decide your shading rhythm before the paper, not during item 40. Two workable rhythms exist. Shade as you go, every item, so the number in the booklet and the number on the question paper never drift. Or mark answers on the question paper and transfer in blocks of ten. Mixing the two mid-paper is how drifts start. Pick one and drill it on homework objectives until your hand is bored.",
      "If you skip a hard item, you must skip the bubble as well or leave a light mark you will not confuse with a final shade. The classic error is skipping on the question paper and shading the next answer in the previous bubble. When you return, the whole block is one step wrong. Prevention: put a small dot beside the number you skipped, on both the question and the answer sheet if rules allow a light mark, and never shade a blank skip.",
      "If you skip a hard item, skip the bubble or leave a light mark you will not confuse with a final shade. The classic drift is skipping on the question paper and shading the next answer in the previous row. A dot beside the skipped number on both sheets, if rules allow a light mark, is cheaper than a whole block one step wrong.",
      "Timing should leave five minutes that are only for the sheet. For a paper of 40 items in 45 minutes, that is not 45 minutes of thinking. That is about 50 seconds an item plus a buffer. Wear a watch if you are allowed, or use the hall clock. At halfway, you should be near item 20, not item 8 and not item 35. Rushing the last fifteen because you admired item 4 is how shading becomes a blur.",
      "Read the stem to the end. Not, except, and all of the above change the job. Circle those words. Then look at the options. Students who shade from a familiar first line choose the option that matches a true sentence the question did not ask. Careless in this sense is not a bad character. It is an unfinished read. The unfinished read is more common than a bubble in the wrong row, and it looks the same on the result slip: a wrong letter.",
      "Changing answers needs a rule. Change only if you found a misread word or a calculation error you can point to. Do not change because another option looks more like something a teacher once said in a different chapter. End-of-paper changes are often fatigue. Fatigue likes a different letter because it feels like effort. Effort is not a reason. A pointed error is a reason.",
      "When you change, cancel fully according to the instruction on the paper. Half-shaded two bubbles are often marked wrong. A clean erase or a clear strike-through, then one new shade, is the only change that exists. Practise the cancel on spare objective sheets. A change you have never physically done will look messy in the last five minutes. The last five minutes are a sweep, not extra philosophy. Run a finger down the numbers. Confirm that item 17's bubble is in row 17. Hunt blank rows. Hunt double shades. Transfer any remaining answers from the question paper if that was your rhythm. Do not start a new debate with item 9 if the sheet still has a hole at 33. Holes are unmarked knowledge. Fill holes first.",
      "## The last five minutes are the sheet",
      "Change an answer only if you can point to a misread word or a calculation error. End-of-paper changes because another option looks fresher are fatigue. Cancel fully. Two half-shaded bubbles are often marked wrong.",
      "Estimation saves calculation items. If a perimeter cannot be 6 cm for a field you would walk, you have a unit error. If a percentage of 80 cannot be 200, you have a decimal error. Estimate, then shade. Estimation is faster than redoing every sum, and it catches the options that were written for the common slip.",
      "Homework must use a real or drawn answer grid sometimes. Practising only by circling letters in the textbook trains a different sport. The sport you will play is a separate sheet with rows. Once a week, sit 15 items with a grid. Include one skip. Include one change. Mark the grid for alignment, not only for content. Alignment is the careless part you came here to reduce.",
      "If the instruction says use HB pencil, use it. Ink that cannot be changed and pencils too faint to scan are both shading errors of a mechanical kind. Carry two pencils and an eraser that actually erases. Test the eraser on a spare margin before the paper if you are allowed. A greasy smear that makes two bubbles look filled is a preventable zero.",
      "This week, sit fifteen items on a drawn grid. Include one skip and one change. Mark alignment as well as content. Carry two HB pencils and an eraser that actually erases."
