      "Match the task to the noise. When the mill runs, do retrieval: cover the page and say steps. When the house is briefly softer, do new work. Insisting that all work needs a cathedral pushes you to midnight, and midnight steals the next day’s lessons you already paid for with attendance.",
      "Pack the bag as a start ritual. Book, exercise book, pen, past questions for the one task, water if you need it. Pack it after each session so the next session begins in ten minutes. Unpacked bags turn twenty-five minutes into fifteen minutes of searching and ten minutes of work. Searching is not a micro-session. It is how noise wins, because during the search you are available for errands.",
      "Use the noises as bells if you must. When the mill runs, do retrieval: cover the page and say steps. Retrieval survives noise better than learning a new theorem. When the house is briefly softer, do the new work. Matching the task to the noise is a skill. Students who insist that all work needs a cathedral only work at midnight. Midnight steals the next day's lessons. Lessons are hours you already paid for with attendance.",
      "Tell one person the end time of the block. Auntie, I am on a timer until half past. End times are easier to respect than a mood. If an errand is truly urgent, pause the timer and restart. A paused timer is still a plan. An abandoned afternoon because someone asked for salt is how people conclude they cannot study at home. They can. They need a restart rule.",
      "Body position matters in a crowded room. A table beats a bed. A bed becomes sleep or a phone. If there is no table, a corner of a dining board with your feet on the floor is enough. Keep the page at an angle you can write. Pain is noise of another kind. Stretch between micro-sessions. Two sessions with a stretch beat one long session that ends in a headache and a surrendered evening.",
      "If a safer quiet place exists two days a week, use it for timed papers. Home micro-sessions cover the rest. Pride about studying in hell is not a mark. Completed tasks are.",
      "Family can be recruited with a visible sign: a book on its side, a card that says timer. Visible signs are kinder than snapping. Snapping starts a story about disrespect. A sign starts a habit. If the household will not honour any sign, take the bag to a compound wall, a school compound after hours if it is safe and allowed, a church steps, a teacher's extra class. The method travels. The fantasy of a suddenly quiet house does not. No extra hours means you steal minutes that already exist: twenty before extra class, fifteen after washing, ten on the bus if you can read without vomiting. Those minutes want retrieval and lists, not new textbooks. New textbooks need a table. Lists of formulae, spoken essays, and covered diagrams can live in a queue. Queues are noisy. They are also free time you currently spend rereading the same chat.",
